# Claude YOLO Quota Watchdog (`claude-yolo-quota-watchdog`)

Running Claude Code in hands-free autonomous YOLO mode (`claude-yolo` / `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions`) enables rapid progress, but risks rapid 5-hour rate limit exhaustion, runaway file churn, and terminal stdout flooding.

This skill establishes circuit breakers and pacing rules for uninterrupted autonomous execution.

---

## ⚡ 1. The 5-Hour Exhaustion Wait-for-Refresh Rule

- If rolling 5-hour rate limits drop to **$\le 10\%$**, do NOT abort or fail the task.
- Stage complete working state, modified diffs, and the active hypothesis to `.agents/memory/activeContext.md`.
- Safely sleep until the recorded reset timestamp passes.
- Upon wakeup, verify quota replenishment and seamlessly resume autonomous execution without asking the user for direction.

---

## 🛑 2. The 3-Strike File Churn Circuit Breaker

- If an edit fails or tests fail 3 consecutive times on the same file, **HALT immediate code edits**.
- Re-read error logs from root cause principles (`systematic-debugging`).
- Formulate a new hypothesis tree before modifying any file a 4th time.

---

## 🛡️ 3. Terminal Stdout Line Ceilings

- Long-running playtests, compilations, and searches must never flood the Claude conversation transcript with thousands of output lines.
- Intercept noisy commands with `token-guard`:
  ```bash
  token-guard make -j$(nproc)
  token-guard git log -n 50
  ```
- Pipe full outputs to `/dev/shm/<job>.log` and inspect only `tail -n 25`.

## Windows 10/11 & Cross-Platform Parity (`windows-linux-cross-os-runtime-bridge`)
- Compatible with both Linux and Windows 10/11 (`install.ps1` / `.cmd` shims, `Path(tempfile.gettempdir())`, and native toolchain resolution).

